INTNET_NAME=agent-net
AGENT_NET=10.77.0.0/24
PI_IP=10.77.0.1
AGENT_IP=10.77.0.10
VBOX_NAT_NET=10.0.2.0/24
VBOX_NAT_GW=10.0.2.2
# The management NIC of the agent, unbridged lab only. A real host-only
# network (vboxnet on the lab host), not an intnet. The host can reach
# it, so vagrant ssh has a path in that is not the NAT adapter. Thus the
# NAT adapter can be cut after that is proved -- the same marker+trigger
# that the bridged mode uses. It reaches the host and the mgmt NICs of the
# other agents, and nothing else. The ip_forward setting of the host controls
# if it reaches the internet. This repo does not control that setting -- see
# docs/risk_model.md. LAB_ prefix because apply.sh sources
# this file. Must not overlap AGENT_NET. 192.168.56.0/24 is the default
# host-only range of VirtualBox, so it needs no extra host setup on a
# fresh install.
LAB_MGMT_NET=192.168.56.0/24
LAB_MGMT_IP=192.168.56.10
# The desk seat, as a plain segment (docs/user_guide/pi_setup.md). On the
# real Pi, it is an 802.1Q child of the agent cable. In the local lab, it
# is a second VirtualBox internal network, because an intnet carries no
# tags. All above the link layer is the same: apply reads
# segments.d/desk.env in both cases, and an IF= with no dot there is a plain
# ethernet port. The four DESK_* lines after the house lines are for the
# lab. The Vagrantfile, vm/desk, vm/itest and the lab part of provision.sh
# read them.
# The house segment in the local lab (docs/user_guide/home_network.md). On the
# real Pi, a person writes house.env by hand for a USB adapter with a cable
# to the LAN port of the access point. Here, provision.sh generates it for a
# third internal network. LAB_ prefix because apply.sh sources this file,
# and apply.env from `pf render` owns the HOUSE_* names with no prefix.
# The MAC is pinned so that the DHCP reservation in house.hosts can name it.
LAB_HOUSE_INTNET=house-net
LAB_HOUSE_NET=10.78.0.0/24
LAB_HOUSE_IP=10.78.0.1
LAB_HOUSE_ADMIN_IP=10.78.0.20
LAB_HOUSE_MAC=08:00:27:a1:78:01
# A second provider for the house. The point of pf dns over dnsmasq is
# that each segment has its own upstream. The suite asserts that the two
# do not share one. A real Pi usually keeps HOUSE_DNS_UPSTREAM empty
# and shares DNS_UPSTREAM, which would make that assertion vacuous.
# It also keeps house lookups away from the address that throttles the
# flood tests.
LAB_HOUSE_DNS_UPSTREAM=1.1.1.1
DESK_INTNET=desk-net
DESK_NET=10.79.0.0/30
DESK_IP=10.79.0.1
DESK_SEAT_IP=10.79.0.2
DNS_UPSTREAM=9.9.9.9
# The two interfaces. Empty is the default: INT_IF is the interface that
# holds PI_IP, and WAN_IF is the interface of the default route. Thus apply
# follows a moved cable automatically. Set the names here to pin them.
# PF_INT_IF and PF_WAN_IF in the environment override both.
INT_IF=
WAN_IF=
# Upstream DNS over TLS (docs/user_guide/pi_setup.md). Empty is the plaintext
# forward to DNS_UPSTREAM above. Set both to send it through the pf dns
# stub instead, for example DOT_AUTH_NAME=dns.quad9.net and
# DOT_IPS='9.9.9.9 149.112.112.112', or a Control D profile:
# DOT_AUTH_NAME=<id>.dns.controld.com and the addresses of that name.
# Put them in net.env.local, which a ship keeps. apply refuses, and does
# not use plaintext instead. The house can use a different provider:
# HOUSE_DOT_AUTH_NAME and HOUSE_DOT_IPS in house.env. These need the two
# keys above set also (docs/user_guide/home_network.md).
DOT_AUTH_NAME=
DOT_IPS=
# How pf dns answers the agent segment. forward resolves upstream, and the
# agent may open TCP 443 only to an address that it got in an answer. fakeip
# never forwards: each A answer is a synthetic address from FAKEIP_NET,
# one for each name. The agent can open 443 only to those (plus
# PI_IP, the lab echo range and direct_ip.allow). The broker
# resolves and dials the real name from the Pi, where the address
# blocklist now applies to its own connections. A lookup alone no longer
# leaves the Pi. This closes DNS names as an exfil channel.
# fakeip is the default since 2026-09-27 (the fakeip lab group and the
# full lab passed on the Pi, and the agent-side HTTP clients dial the
# pool). Pin AGENT_DNS=forward in
# net.env.local for real answers.
#
# With fakeip, no name that the agent resolves gives a real address. Thus
# SSH to a host in ssh_allow.ips must use the address, not the name. That
# rule is address-based and did not change.
#
# FAKEIP_NET is the pool: an IPv4 network from /16 to /24, inside
# 10/8, 100.64/10, 172.16/12, 192.168/16 or 198.18/15 (ranges the broker
# is already refused from). It must not overlap AGENT_NET, HOUSE_NET or
# a segments.d NET. If it does, apply refuses, in both modes.
AGENT_DNS=fakeip
FAKEIP_NET=198.18.0.0/16
ALLOW_DOMAIN=example.com
BLOCK_DOMAIN=pi-fortress-block.test
BLOCK_IP=192.0.2.1
ECHO_CORE_IP=192.0.2.2
ECHO_OPEN_IP=192.0.2.3
# The uplink (WAN_IF) is a network that you do not control, for example
# cafe or hotel Wi-Fi. 1 closes SSH and ping on it, and stops outgoing mDNS
# and LLMNR. apply refuses unless a house admin or an ADMIN=yes segment can still
# reach the Pi. 0 leaves SSH open on the uplink (docs/user_guide/public_wifi.md).
UNTRUSTED_UPLINK=0
# The nightly blocklist feed refresh (pi-fortress-fetch.timer). 1 installs
# and enables it. With 0, feeds refresh only in a manual pf apply, as
# before the timer existed. The timer never restarts the fence. Thus the
# SNI/Host check keeps the list that it started with until an operator
# applies. The Feeds screen shows when that is pending.
FEED_REFRESH=1
# The hardware watchdog (lib/harden.sh harden_watchdog). systemd feeds it,
# and it resets a Pi that hangs, for example when RAM is exhausted. After
# the boot, pf alert posts that the Pi restarted without a clean shutdown.
# auto enables it when the kernel has a watchdog device (the Pi 5 has one,
# a lab VM usually not). 1 enables it always, 0 disables it. A full apply
# writes /etc/systemd/system.conf.d/pi-fortress-watchdog.conf.
HW_WATCHDOG=auto
# The per-slot hourly budget (docs/fence.md 5.2d). For each credential
# slot, the broker keeps a sliding hour of the requests into which it
# substituted, and of the request-body MB that went out with them. These
# four are the lines that it compares that hour with. 0 disables that line.
#
# A warn writes one operator row for each crossing. That row goes to pf alert,
# the Events screen and the webhook. The slot continues to work. A stop
# quarantines the slot exactly as the x key on the Secrets screen does. Thus
# the next request with that placeholder gets the same refusal as each
# disabled slot, and R restores it. Both stops ship off: a hard stop
# that fires on a false positive kills a session that works. Only the
# operator knows the honest rate of their agent. Read the
# warn rows for a week first, then set the stop above what you saw.
#
# The window is in the memory of the broker, so a restart starts the hour
# again. apply restarts the broker, so a change here takes effect on the
# next apply.
SLOT_WARN_REQUESTS=3000
SLOT_STOP_REQUESTS=0
SLOT_WARN_UPLOAD_MB=500
SLOT_STOP_UPLOAD_MB=0
# Connection budgets per device and per segment (internal/render/connlimit.go,
# docs/user_guide/pi_setup.md "Connection limits"). Without them, one device could
# open flows until the kernel connection table was full. All other segments
# and SSH into the Pi share that table.
#   *_CONN_PER_IP  live connections one address may hold
#   *_NEW_PER_IP   new connections a second one address may open, RATE/BURST
#   *_CONN_TOTAL   live connections the whole segment may hold
#   HOUSE_NEW_TOTAL new TCP connections a second the whole house may open,
#                  RATE/BURST. Many addresses under the per-device rate
#                  would otherwise fill the table with closed flows
#                  (the agent's total is fixed at 300/600)
#   SEG_NEW_TOTAL  the same for each plain segment, RATE/BURST: 300/600
#                  is 9600 closed flows at worst per segment (7 % of the
#                  table), so a few segments stay small next to the house
# AGENT_ is the agent segment (TCP to the Pi: the engine and pf www), HOUSE_
# the house, SEG_ each plain segment in segments.d (the desk seat too). A
# segment file may set CONN_PER_IP, NEW_PER_IP, CONN_TOTAL or
# NEW_TOTAL. Each value that it does not set takes the SEG_ value here. The
# SEG_ values are the agent values: the desk is the host of the agent VMs, so
# an agent that escapes its VM should not find a bigger budget there.
# 0 disables one limit. An over-limit flow is refused (a TCP reset) and
# logged as "<segment>-connlimit". Connections that are open are never cut.
AGENT_CONN_PER_IP=128
AGENT_NEW_PER_IP=50/200
AGENT_CONN_TOTAL=4096
HOUSE_CONN_PER_IP=1024
HOUSE_NEW_PER_IP=100/500
HOUSE_CONN_TOTAL=32768
HOUSE_NEW_TOTAL=1000/5000
SEG_CONN_PER_IP=128
SEG_NEW_PER_IP=50/200
SEG_CONN_TOTAL=4096
SEG_NEW_TOTAL=300/600
